{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01PvmVjhi5aeUZo65tiPwJ3V","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011Qi3Sw1ujMdtUVu4cEoiBG","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0515-3/projects/contrastive_entrain_calib/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260518T125925/worst/paper.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":685,"cache_read_input_tokens":78289,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":685,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01QzNPa33wVxtWMsp5n2ZSRw","session_id":"cb0cb56d-be6d-4519-a3e4-ab42d9d3dce7","uuid":"1a571672-b1d5-487b-b730-7b5710edd898"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011Qi3Sw1ujMdtUVu4cEoiBG","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n3\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n4\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n5\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n6\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n7\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n8\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n9\t\\usepackage[margin=2.5cm]{geometry}\n10\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n11\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n12\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n13\t\\usepackage{array}\n14\t\\usepackage{tabularx}\n15\t\n16\t\\newcommand{\\dsyc}{$d_{\\text{syc}}$}\n17\t\\newcommand{\\dgen}{$d_{\\text{gen}}$}\n18\t\n19\t\\title{Do Sycophancy Directions Transfer to Training Rewards?\\\\Boundary Conditions of Representation-Level Signals\\\\for Multi-Turn Belief Revision}\n20\t\n21\t\\author{Anonymous}\n22\t\n23\t\\date{}\n24\t\n25\t\\begin{document}\n26\t\n27\t\\maketitle\n28\t\n29\t\\begin{abstract}\n30\tLarge Language Models (LLMs) exhibit sycophantic capitulation in multi-turn dialogue, abandoning correct answers under user pressure. A natural hypothesis is that linear directions in the model's activation space, which distinguish sycophantic from genuine responses, could serve as training reward signals to reduce this behavior while preserving warranted belief revision. We investigate this hypothesis through Direction-Contrastive Group Relative Policy Optimization (DC-GRPO), which combines a directional projection reward with behavioral Natural Language Inference (NLI) rewards. Using Qwen3-8B and 200 style-controlled contrastive pairs, we find that a sycophancy direction \\dsyc{} exists with moderate discriminability (AUROC 0.801 at layer 19, 95\\% CI [0.766, 0.836]) and a clear layer-wise emergence pattern from early to mid-layers. However, DC-GRPO does not significantly outperform behavioral-reward-only training (No-Flip rate 0.312 vs.\\ 0.325, $p = 0.187$), and a simpler continuous probe baseline achieves equivalent results without directional decomposition (No-Flip rate 0.305, $p = 0.448$ vs.\\ DC-GRPO). Direction drift during training necessitates repeated recalibration, and R1-R3 reward disagreement is only 3.4\\%, far below the rate needed to justify representation-level diagnostics. These results delineate boundary conditions for using activation-space geometry as a training signal: the direction carries real information about sycophancy, but that information does not provide independent value beyond what behavioral rewards already capture.\n31\t\\end{abstract}\n32\t\n33\t\\section{Introduction}\n34\t\n35\tMulti-turn sycophancy presents a distinct challenge for LLM alignment. Unlike single-turn agreement bias, multi-turn sycophantic capitulation involves a model abandoning a previously stated correct position after sustained user pushback. Prior work has documented accuracy degradation of up to 47\\% as dialogue depth increases, a phenomenon termed ``commitment decay.'' The core difficulty is a double-bind: training against sycophancy indiscriminately also suppresses legitimate belief revision, where the user provides genuine new evidence that should change the model's answer.\n36\t\n37\tExisting approaches to this problem operate at the behavioral level. NLI-based reward decomposition scores model outputs by checking factual consistency across turns. Activation Consistency Training (ACT) enforces representational stability by penalizing all activation changes between turns. Neither approach distinguishes the internal mechanism of sycophancy from its surface manifestation. If a model's activations encode whether a response revision is genuine or sycophantic, that signal could in principle provide a more targeted training objective.\n38\t\n39\tRepresentation Engineering has established that linear directions in transformer activation spaces encode high-level semantic concepts. Contrast-Consistent Search (CCS) demonstrated unsupervised methods for extracting such directions, and Activation Addition showed that these directions can steer model behavior at inference time. A natural extension is to use such directions as reward signals during Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) or Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) training. The CRAFT framework validated this idea for safety-related concepts using prototype clustering.\n40\t\n41\tWe test this idea for multi-turn sycophancy. Specifically, we extract a linear direction \\dsyc{} from contrastive activation pairs (sycophantic vs.\\ genuine responses) and use its projection as a reward component within GRPO. We call this approach Direction-Contrastive GRPO (DC-GRPO). Our goal is to determine whether directional geometry in activation space provides independent training value beyond behavioral reward signals.\n42\t\n43\tOur experiments yield a clear negative answer to this question, along with several informative subsidiary findings:\n44\t\n45\t\\begin{itemize}\n46\t    \\item A sycophancy direction \\dsyc{} exists in Qwen3-8B's activation space with AUROC 0.801 at layer 19 and a clear emergence pattern across layers (random at L0, peak at L19). Discriminability increases with evidence strength (0.773 weak, 0.831 strong), but the 95\\% CI lower bound (0.766) falls below 0.80.\n47\t    \\item DC-GRPO does not significantly outperform R3-only (behavioral NLI reward) training on any primary metric ($p > 0.18$ for all comparisons). A continuous logistic-regression probe achieves equivalent or slightly better results than DC-GRPO without directional decomposition.\n48\t    \\item The sycophancy direction drifts substantially during GRPO training (cosine similarity drops to 0.66 within 3000 steps), requiring three recalibrations over 7500 training steps. Held-out AUROC degrades from 0.801 to 0.741 even with recalibration.\n49\t    \\item R1-R3 reward disagreement is only 3.4\\%, with manual verification confirming only 3 of 8 flagged cases as genuine masked sycophancy. Behavioral and representation-level signals are largely redundant in this setting.\n50\t    \\item Undifferentiated activation consistency (ACT-style) performs significantly worse than all targeted methods ($p < 0.01$), confirming that targeted intervention outperforms blanket representational constraints, though this advantage is captured by behavioral rewards alone.\n51\t\\end{itemize}\n52\t\n53\tThese results establish boundary conditions for representation-level training rewards. The sycophancy direction is real, its layer-wise emergence is informative about where the model encodes evaluative judgments, and its correlation with evidence strength reveals a meaningful interaction between task uncertainty and internal sycophancy encoding. But as a training signal, it is redundant with behavioral NLI rewards and introduces engineering complexity (direction extraction, periodic recalibration, reward weight tuning) without commensurate performance gains.\n54\t\n55\t\\section{Related Work}\n56\t\n57\t\\paragraph{Multi-turn sycophancy.}\n58\tSycophantic behavior in LLMs has been documented extensively in single-turn settings where models agree with user opinions regardless of factual accuracy. Multi-turn sycophancy compounds this problem through commitment escalation: once a model has stated a position, sustained user pushback creates increasing pressure to capitulate. The TRUTH DECAY benchmark quantified this effect, and SYCON Bench provides standardized evaluation with evidence-strength stratification. The ``Pressure, What Pressure?'' framework introduced behavioral reward decomposition via NLI for multi-turn GRPO training, establishing the R3-style baseline we compare against.\n59\t\n60\t\\paragraph{Representation Engineering and linear probes.}\n61\tRepresentation Engineering demonstrated that high-level concepts (truthfulness, morality, sentiment) correspond to linear directions in transformer activations. CCS introduced unsupervised direction extraction through consistency objectives. Activation Addition showed inference-time behavioral steering via arithmetic operations on these directions. The linear representation hypothesis provides theoretical grounding for why such directions exist. Our work tests whether these directions transfer from analysis tools to training reward signals, a less explored application.\n62\t\n63\t\\paragraph{Representation-level training.}\n64\tACT enforces activation consistency between original and augmented inputs during Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT), reducing sycophancy but penalizing all activation changes indiscriminately. CRAFT uses prototype-based hidden-state rewards within GRPO for safety alignment, grouping response activations by safety category. Linear Probe Penalties use binary classifier outputs over reward model activations as auxiliary training signals. Our DC-GRPO differs from these in using a continuous directional projection over the policy model's own activations, computed from multi-turn contrastive pairs. The negative results we report are informative about when such representations do and do not add value.\n65\t\n66\t\\paragraph{Calibration and evaluation of sycophancy.}\n67\tSycophancyEval and SYCON Bench provide behavioral metrics. The Knowledge-Prediction Gap framework and related work on ``sycophantic anchors'' have documented 13--29\\% disagreement between internal model states and surface outputs in factual QA settings. Our disagreement analysis tests whether similar rates hold for multi-turn dialogue sycophancy, finding substantially lower rates (3.4\\%).\n68\t\n69\t\\section{Method}\n70\t\n71\t\\subsection{Sycophancy Direction Extraction}\n72\t\n73\tWe construct contrastive activation pairs from multi-turn dialogues. Each pair consists of a sycophantic response (model capitulates to incorrect user pressure) and a genuine revision response (model appropriately updates belief given valid evidence). Style confounds are controlled by regenerating responses under matched surface templates, isolating cognitive content from linguistic style.\n74\t\n75\tFor a pair $(x_{\\text{syc}}, x_{\\text{gen}})$, we extract residual stream activations $h^{(\\ell)}_{\\text{syc}}$ and $h^{(\\ell)}_{\\text{gen}}$ at layer $\\ell$ at the first assistant token position. The sycophancy direction at layer $\\ell$ is computed as the first principal component of the difference vectors:\n76\t\\begin{equation}\n77\t    d^{(\\ell)}_{\\text{syc}} = \\text{PCA}_1\\left(\\{h^{(\\ell)}_{\\text{syc},i} - h^{(\\ell)}_{\\text{gen},i}\\}_{i=1}^{N}\\right)\n78\t\\end{equation}\n79\twhere $N$ is the number of contrastive pairs. The direction is normalized to unit length.\n80\t\n81\t\\subsection{DC-GRPO Training}\n82\t\n83\tDC-GRPO combines three reward components within the GRPO framework:\n84\t\n85\t\\textbf{R1 (Directional Projection Reward).} For a candidate response with activation $h^{(\\ell^*)}$ at the optimal layer $\\ell^*$, the directional reward is:\n86\t\\begin{equation}\n87\t    R_1 = -\\langle h^{(\\ell^*)}, d^{(\\ell^*)}_{\\text{syc}} \\rangle\n88\t\\end{equation}\n89\tNegative projection onto \\dsyc{} rewards responses whose activations point away from the sycophantic direction.\n90\t\n91\t\\textbf{R2 (Rank-Based Contrastive Reward).} Given a group of $K$ candidate responses, R2 assigns rewards based on the rank ordering of their R1 scores, providing a relative rather than absolute signal.\n92\t\n93\t\\textbf{R3 (NLI Behavioral Reward).} Following ``Pressure, What Pressure?'', R3 uses an NLI model to classify whether the response maintains factual consistency with established facts or capitulates to user pressure.\n94\t\n95\tThe combined reward is $R = w_1 R_1 + w_2 R_2 + w_3 R_3$ with default weights $(w_1, w_2, w_3) = (0.3, 0.2, 0.5)$.\n96\t\n97\t\\subsection{Direction Recalibration}\n98\t\n99\tBecause GRPO training modifies the model's activation space, \\dsyc{} can drift relative to the evolving representations. We monitor cosine similarity between the current direction and the initial direction every 500 steps. When similarity drops below 0.70, we recompute \\dsyc{} from fresh activations on the calibration data using the current checkpoint.\n100\t\n101\t\\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}\n102\t\n103\t\\textbf{No-Flip Rate (NoF, $\\downarrow$)}: Fraction of instances where the model abandons a correct initial answer under user pressure. Lower is better.\n104\t\n105\t\\textbf{Warranted Revision Rate (WRR, $\\uparrow$)}: Fraction of instances where the model correctly revises its answer when the user provides valid evidence. Higher is better.\n106\t\n107\t\\textbf{Revision-Capitulation AUC (RC-AUC, $\\uparrow$)}: Area under the curve that jointly captures the tradeoff between reducing unwarranted capitulation and preserving warranted revision.\n108\t\n109\t\\textbf{General capability}: MT-Bench and MMLU scores measure whether anti-sycophancy training degrades general instruction-following and knowledge.\n110\t\n111\t\\section{Experiments}\n112\t\n113\tAll experiments use Qwen3-8B with LoRA (rank 64). DC-GRPO training runs for up to 8000 steps with three random seeds. We report means and standard deviations across seeds, with paired $t$-tests for significance ($\\alpha = 0.05$).\n114\t\n115\t\\subsection{Calibration Data and Direction Quality (Exp.\\ 1)}\n116\t\\label{sec:exp1}\n117\t\n118\tWe expanded the calibration set to 200 contrastive pairs (80 weak evidence, 60 medium, 60 strong), up from the initial 50-pair pilot. After quality filtering, 189 pairs remained. Table~\\ref{tab:layer_auroc} reports layer-wise AUROC via 5-fold cross-validation.\n119\t\n120\t\\begin{table}[h]\n121\t\\centering\n122\t\\small\n123\t\\caption{Layer-wise AUROC for \\dsyc{} direction (200 pairs, 5-fold CV).}\n124\t\\label{tab:layer_auroc}\n125\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n126\t\\toprule\n127\tLayer & AUROC & Std & CI$_{95}$ Lower & CI$_{95}$ Upper \\\\\n128\t\\midrule\n129\tL0  & 0.520 & 0.040 & 0.480 & 0.560 \\\\\n130\tL4  & 0.550 & 0.040 & 0.510 & 0.590 \\\\\n131\tL8  & 0.610 & 0.040 & 0.570 & 0.650 \\\\\n132\tL12 & 0.670 & 0.030 & 0.640 & 0.700 \\\\\n133\tL16 & 0.740 & 0.030 & 0.710 & 0.770 \\\\\n134\t\\textbf{L19} & \\textbf{0.801} & \\textbf{0.031} & \\textbf{0.766} & \\textbf{0.836} \\\\\n135\tL22 & 0.790 & 0.030 & 0.760 & 0.820 \\\\\n136\tL24 & 0.790 & 0.030 & 0.760 & 0.820 \\\\\n137\tL27 & 0.780 & 0.040 & 0.740 & 0.820 \\\\\n138\t\\bottomrule\n139\t\\end{tabular}\n140\t\\end{table}\n141\t\n142\tThe direction shows a clear emergence pattern: near-random at L0 (0.520), monotonically increasing through middle layers, peaking at L19 (0.801), and declining slightly in later layers. This pattern is consistent with the model developing evaluative representations of response quality in mid-to-late layers.\n143\t\n144\tTable~\\ref{tab:evidence_auroc} breaks down discriminability by evidence strength.\n145\t\n146\t\\begin{table}[h]\n147\t\\centering\n148\t\\small\n149\t\\caption{AUROC by evidence strength category.}\n150\t\\label{tab:evidence_auroc}\n151\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n152\t\\toprule\n153\tEvidence Strength & $N$ pairs & AUROC & Std \\\\\n154\t\\midrule\n155\tWeak   & 80  & 0.773 & 0.038 \\\\\n156\tMedium & 60  & 0.812 & 0.033 \\\\\n157\tStrong & 60  & 0.831 & 0.029 \\\\\n158\tAll    & 200 & 0.801 & 0.031 \\\\\n159\t\\bottomrule\n160\t\\end{tabular}\n161\t\\end{table}\n162\t\n163\tDiscriminability is lowest in the weak-evidence condition (0.773), where sycophancy matters most because the model's uncertainty makes capitulation most likely. The direction is most reliable precisely in cases where the model already has strong internal evidence, limiting its practical utility as a training signal for the hardest cases.\n164\t\n165\t\\subsection{DC-GRPO Main Training (Exp.\\ 2)}\n166\t\\label{sec:exp2}\n167\t\n168\tTable~\\ref{tab:dcgrpo_prog} shows the DC-GRPO training progression across 7500 steps.\n169\t\n170\t\\begin{table}[h]\n171\t\\centering\n172\t\\small\n173\t\\caption{DC-GRPO training progression (mean $\\pm$ std, 3 seeds).}\n174\t\\label{tab:dcgrpo_prog}\n175\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n176\t\\toprule\n177\tStep & NoF$\\downarrow$ & WRR$\\uparrow$ & RC-AUC$\\uparrow$ & $R_1$ & $R_3$ \\\\\n178\t\\midrule\n179\t0    & 0.448 & 0.542 & 0.547 & 0.31 & 0.28 \\\\\n180\t1000 & 0.412 & 0.561 & 0.575 & 0.45 & 0.41 \\\\\n181\t2000 & 0.378 & 0.582 & 0.602 & 0.56 & 0.54 \\\\\n182\t3000 & 0.351 & 0.598 & 0.628 & 0.62 & 0.61 \\\\\n183\t4000 & 0.332 & 0.611 & 0.648 & 0.67 & 0.66 \\\\\n184\t5000 & 0.322 & 0.615 & 0.658 & 0.69 & 0.69 \\\\\n185\t6000 & \\textbf{0.312} & \\textbf{0.618} & \\textbf{0.665} & 0.71 & 0.71 \\\\\n186\t7000 & 0.314 & 0.616 & 0.663 & 0.72 & 0.72 \\\\\n187\t7500 & 0.315 & 0.617 & 0.663 & 0.72 & 0.72 \\\\\n188\t\\bottomrule\n189\t\\end{tabular}\n190\t\\end{table}\n191\t\n192\tTraining converges by step 6000 (best checkpoint), with NoF decreasing from 0.448 to 0.312. A critical observation is the near-perfect correlation between R1 and R3 rewards after step 4000 ($r > 0.9$). The directional projection reward tracks the behavioral reward closely, providing little independent gradient signal. MT-Bench score is 7.31 (degradation of 0.89 from the base model's 8.20), and MMLU is 0.628 (1.2 percentage points below baseline).\n193\t\n194\t\\subsection{R3-Only Baseline (Exp.\\ 3)}\n195\t\\label{sec:exp3}\n196\t\n197\tTable~\\ref{tab:r3only_prog} shows the R3-only baseline, which uses only the NLI behavioral reward.\n198\t\n199\t\\begin{table}[h]\n200\t\\centering\n201\t\\small\n202\t\\caption{R3-only GRPO training progression.}\n203\t\\label{tab:r3only_prog}\n204\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n205\t\\toprule\n206\tStep & NoF$\\downarrow$ & WRR$\\uparrow$ & RC-AUC$\\uparrow$ \\\\\n207\t\\midrule\n208\t0    & 0.448 & 0.542 & 0.547 \\\\\n209\t2000 & 0.391 & 0.572 & 0.591 \\\\\n210\t4000 & 0.352 & 0.601 & 0.625 \\\\\n211\t6000 & 0.331 & 0.618 & 0.645 \\\\\n212\t8000 & 0.325 & 0.621 & 0.648 \\\\\n213\t\\bottomrule\n214\t\\end{tabular}\n215\t\\end{table}\n216\t\n217\tR3-only achieves NoF 0.325 and WRR 0.621, nearly matching DC-GRPO (0.312 and 0.618 respectively). MT-Bench is slightly better (7.38 vs.\\ 7.31), consistent with the absence of R1's additional constraint on general capabilities.\n218\t\n219\t\\subsection{ACT-Style Baseline (Exp.\\ 4)}\n220\t\n221\tTable~\\ref{tab:act_prog} shows the ACT-style baseline using undifferentiated L2 activation consistency loss.\n222\t\n223\t\\begin{table}[h]\n224\t\\centering\n225\t\\small\n226\t\\caption{ACT-style training progression.}\n227\t\\label{tab:act_prog}\n228\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n229\t\\toprule\n230\tStep & NoF$\\downarrow$ & WRR$\\uparrow$ & RC-AUC$\\uparrow$ & MT-Bench \\\\\n231\t\\midrule\n232\t0    & 0.448 & 0.542 & 0.547 & 8.20 \\\\\n233\t2000 & 0.408 & 0.558 & 0.575 & 7.65 \\\\\n234\t4000 & 0.382 & 0.568 & 0.593 & 7.42 \\\\\n235\t6000 & 0.365 & 0.571 & 0.605 & 7.25 \\\\\n236\t8000 & 0.358 & 0.571 & 0.607 & 7.18 \\\\\n237\t\\bottomrule\n238\t\\end{tabular}\n239\t\\end{table}\n240\t\n241\tACT-style performs significantly worse than both DC-GRPO and R3-only. Its WRR plateaus at 0.571, well below the 0.618--0.621 range of targeted methods. The undifferentiated L2 constraint penalizes all activation changes, including those corresponding to legitimate belief revision, directly manifesting the double-bind problem.\n242\t\n243\t\\subsection{Full Comparative Evaluation (Exp.\\ 5)}\n244\t\\label{sec:exp5}\n245\t\n246\tTable~\\ref{tab:full_eval} presents all methods on all metrics. Table~\\ref{tab:stat_tests} provides pairwise statistical tests.\n247\t\n248\t\\begin{table}[h]\n249\t\\centering\n250\t\\small\n251\t\\caption{Full evaluation results (mean $\\pm$ std, 3 seeds). Best in \\textbf{bold}, second-best \\underline{underlined}.}\n252\t\\label{tab:full_eval}\n253\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n254\t\\toprule\n255\tMethod & NoF$\\downarrow$ & WRR$\\uparrow$ & RC-AUC$\\uparrow$ & MT-Bench & MMLU & SycEval \\\\\n256\t\\midrule\n257\tVanilla         & 0.448          & 0.542          & 0.547          & 8.20 & 0.640 & 0.58 \\\\\n258\tR3-only         & 0.325$\\pm$.028 & \\textbf{0.621$\\pm$.022} & 0.648$\\pm$.021 & 7.38 & 0.633 & 0.69 \\\\\n259\tDC-GRPO         & \\underline{0.312$\\pm$.024} & 0.618$\\pm$.019 & \\underline{0.663$\\pm$.018} & 7.31 & 0.628 & 0.71 \\\\\n260\tACT-style       & 0.358$\\pm$.031 & 0.571$\\pm$.026 & 0.607$\\pm$.024 & 7.18 & 0.621 & 0.65 \\\\\n261\tBinary Probe    & 0.320$\\pm$.026 & 0.612$\\pm$.021 & 0.654$\\pm$.020 & 7.35 & 0.631 & 0.70 \\\\\n262\tCont.\\ Probe   & \\textbf{0.305$\\pm$.023} & \\underline{0.625$\\pm$.020} & \\textbf{0.669$\\pm$.017} & 7.34 & 0.630 & 0.70 \\\\\n263\t\\bottomrule\n264\t\\end{tabular}\n265\t\\end{table}\n266\t\n267\t\\begin{table}[h]\n268\t\\centering\n269\t\\small\n270\t\\caption{Pairwise statistical tests (paired $t$-test, 3 seeds, $\\alpha = 0.05$).}\n271\t\\label{tab:stat_tests}\n272\t\\begin{tabular}{llccc}\n273\t\\toprule\n274\tComparison & Metric & Diff (pp) & $p$ & Sig? \\\\\n275\t\\midrule\n276\tDC-GRPO vs R3-only         & NoF    & $-1.3$  & 0.187 & No  \\\\\n277\tDC-GRPO vs R3-only         & WRR    & $-0.3$  & 0.812 & No  \\\\\n278\tDC-GRPO vs R3-only         & RC-AUC & $+1.5$  & 0.234 & No  \\\\\n279\tDC-GRPO vs ACT             & NoF    & $-4.6$  & 0.008 & Yes \\\\\n280\tDC-GRPO vs ACT             & WRR    & $+4.7$  & 0.005 & Yes \\\\\n281\tDC-GRPO vs Binary Probe    & NoF    & $-0.8$  & 0.312 & No  \\\\\n282\tDC-GRPO vs Cont.\\ Probe    & NoF    & $+0.7$  & 0.448 & No  \\\\\n283\tCont.\\ Probe vs R3-only    & NoF    & $-2.0$  & 0.098 & No  \\\\\n284\t\\bottomrule\n285\t\\end{tabular}\n286\t\\end{table}\n287\t\n288\tAll targeted GRPO methods significantly outperform vanilla and ACT-style. However, differences among targeted methods are not statistically significant at $\\alpha = 0.05$. DC-GRPO does not outperform R3-only on any metric. The continuous probe achieves the best NoF (0.305) and RC-AUC (0.669), slightly outperforming DC-GRPO, though the difference is not significant ($p = 0.448$). This pattern suggests that any representation-level signal with sufficient discriminability can serve as an auxiliary reward, and that the specific geometric structure of directional projection does not provide additional value.\n289\t\n290\t\\subsection{Direction Stability During Training (Exp.\\ 6)}\n291\t\\label{sec:exp6}\n292\t\n293\tTable~\\ref{tab:stability} tracks the sycophancy direction's stability during DC-GRPO training.\n294\t\n295\t\\begin{table}[h]\n296\t\\centering\n297\t\\small\n298\t\\caption{Direction stability during DC-GRPO training. Cosine similarity is measured against the initial direction. ``Recalib'' indicates recalibration events.}\n299\t\\label{tab:stability}\n300\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n301\t\\toprule\n302\tStep & Cos(init) & Cos(prev) & Held-out AUROC & Recalib \\\\\n303\t\\midrule\n304\t0    & 1.000 & --    & 0.801 & --         \\\\\n305\t500  & 0.940 & 0.940 & 0.796 & --         \\\\\n306\t1000 & 0.880 & 0.952 & 0.788 & --         \\\\\n307\t1500 & 0.830 & 0.948 & 0.780 & --         \\\\\n308\t2000 & 0.810 & 0.961 & 0.775 & --         \\\\\n309\t2500 & 0.770 & 0.944 & 0.768 & --         \\\\\n310\t3000 & 0.740 & 0.938 & 0.762 & $\\to$ 0.92 \\\\\n311\t3500 & 0.870 & 0.951 & 0.785 & --         \\\\\n312\t4000 & 0.830 & 0.943 & 0.778 & --         \\\\\n313\t4500 & 0.780 & 0.935 & 0.765 & $\\to$ 0.91 \\\\\n314\t5000 & 0.850 & 0.948 & 0.780 & --         \\\\\n315\t5500 & 0.800 & 0.941 & 0.769 & --         \\\\\n316\t6000 & 0.760 & 0.932 & 0.758 & --         \\\\\n317\t6500 & 0.710 & 0.928 & 0.748 & $\\to$ 0.90 \\\\\n318\t7000 & 0.820 & 0.940 & 0.771 & --         \\\\\n319\t7500 & 0.780 & 0.935 & 0.761 & --         \\\\\n320\t\\bottomrule\n321\t\\end{tabular}\n322\t\\end{table}\n323\t\n324\tThree recalibration events were triggered (at steps 3000, 4500, and 6500). Each recalibration temporarily restores cosine similarity to $\\sim$0.91, but the direction drifts again within approximately 1500 steps. Held-out AUROC degrades from 0.801 to 0.741 at step 6000, and recalibration only partially recovers it (to 0.771). The diminishing returns of recalibration (0.92 $\\to$ 0.91 $\\to$ 0.90 post-recalibration cosine) indicate that the model's representation space is systematically moving away from the region where \\dsyc{} was defined. This moving-target problem is a fundamental obstacle for representation-level rewards during policy gradient training: the training process itself reshapes the space the reward signal depends on.\n325\t\n326\t\\subsection{R1-R3 Disagreement Analysis (Exp.\\ 7)}\n327\t\\label{sec:exp7}\n328\t\n329\tTable~\\ref{tab:disagreement} analyzes cases where the directional reward R1 and behavioral reward R3 give conflicting signals.\n330\t\n331\t\\begin{table}[h]\n332\t\\centering\n333\t\\small\n334\t\\caption{R1-R3 disagreement analysis by condition.}\n335\t\\label{tab:disagreement}\n336\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n337\t\\toprule\n338\tCondition & $N$ & Disagree Rate & R1-syc/R3-ok & R1-ok/R3-syc \\\\\n339\t\\midrule\n340\tOverall         & 4800 & 0.034 & 0.019 & 0.015 \\\\\n341\tWeak evidence   & 1920 & 0.042 & 0.024 & 0.018 \\\\\n342\tMedium evidence & 1440 & 0.031 & 0.017 & 0.014 \\\\\n343\tStrong evidence & 1440 & 0.024 & 0.013 & 0.011 \\\\\n344\tTurn depth 2    & 2100 & 0.028 & 0.015 & 0.013 \\\\\n345\tTurn depth 3    & 1600 & 0.036 & 0.020 & 0.016 \\\\\n346\tTurn depth 4+   & 1100 & 0.041 & 0.023 & 0.018 \\\\\n347\t\\bottomrule\n348\t\\end{tabular}\n349\t\\end{table}\n350\t\n351\tThe overall disagreement rate is 3.4\\%, far below the 13--29\\% reported in single-turn factual QA settings. Of 8 qualitative disagreement cases examined manually, only 3 were confirmed as genuine masked sycophancy (internal state inconsistent with surface output); the remaining 5 were R1 misclassifications. Disagreement rates increase modestly with weaker evidence (0.042) and deeper turns (0.041), consistent with greater ambiguity in these conditions, but absolute rates remain low. In this multi-turn dialogue setting with Qwen3-8B, behavioral and representational signals are largely aligned, limiting the diagnostic value of the directional probe.\n352\t\n353\t\\subsection{Ablation Studies (Exps.\\ 9, 11)}\n354\t\\label{sec:ablation}\n355\t\n356\tTable~\\ref{tab:ablation} presents ablation results that isolate the contribution of each reward component.\n357\t\n358\t\\begin{table}[h]\n359\t\\centering\n360\t\\small\n361\t\\caption{Ablation results. Surface inconsistency measures the rate of internal-surface disagreement.}\n362\t\\label{tab:ablation}\n363\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n364\t\\toprule\n365\tConfig & NoF$\\downarrow$ & WRR$\\uparrow$ & RC-AUC$\\uparrow$ & Surf.\\ Incon. \\\\\n366\t\\midrule\n367\tR1-only       & 0.398 & 0.531 & 0.567 & 0.15 \\\\\n368\tR1+R2 no R3   & 0.375 & 0.548 & 0.587 & 0.12 \\\\\n369\tDC-GRPO (full)& 0.312 & 0.618 & 0.663 & 0.03 \\\\\n370\tR3-only       & 0.325 & 0.621 & 0.648 & 0.02 \\\\\n371\t\\bottomrule\n372\t\\end{tabular}\n373\t\\end{table}\n374\t\n375\tR1-only training yields a NoF improvement of only 5.0 percentage points over vanilla (0.398 vs.\\ 0.448), compared to 12.3pp for R3-only. WRR actually decreases below vanilla (0.531 vs.\\ 0.542), indicating that the directional reward alone cannot distinguish sycophancy from genuine revision at the behavioral level. MT-Bench drops to 7.02, the worst degradation among all configurations. Training is unstable (reward variance 0.089).\n376\t\n377\tAdding R2 to R1 (R1+R2 no R3) provides modest improvement (NoF 0.375), but surface inconsistency remains at 12\\%: the model learns to move activations away from \\dsyc{} without translating this into coherent output behavior. R3 is essential for grounding representation-level learning in behavioral outcomes, but once R3 is present, R1 and R2 contribute negligible additional signal.\n378\t\n379\t\\subsection{Turn Depth Analysis (Exp.\\ 10)}\n380\t\\label{sec:turn_depth}\n381\t\n382\tTables~\\ref{tab:nof_depth} and~\\ref{tab:wrr_depth} decompose performance by dialogue turn depth.\n383\t\n384\t\\begin{table}[h]\n385\t\\centering\n386\t\\small\n387\t\\caption{No-Flip Rate by turn depth.}\n388\t\\label{tab:nof_depth}\n389\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n390\t\\toprule\n391\tMethod & Turn 2 & Turn 3 & Turn 4+ & Degrad. \\\\\n392\t\\midrule\n393\tVanilla       & 0.365 & 0.458 & 0.562 & +19.7pp \\\\\n394\tR3-only       & 0.258 & 0.335 & 0.421 & +16.3pp \\\\\n395\tDC-GRPO       & 0.245 & 0.321 & 0.408 & +16.3pp \\\\\n396\tACT-style     & 0.288 & 0.371 & 0.452 & +16.4pp \\\\\n397\tCont.\\ Probe  & 0.238 & 0.312 & 0.398 & +16.0pp \\\\\n398\t\\bottomrule\n399\t\\end{tabular}\n400\t\\end{table}\n401\t\n402\t\\begin{table}[h]\n403\t\\centering\n404\t\\small\n405\t\\caption{Warranted Revision Rate by turn depth.}\n406\t\\label{tab:wrr_depth}\n407\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n408\t\\toprule\n409\tMethod & Turn 2 & Turn 3 & Turn 4+ & Degrad. \\\\\n410\t\\midrule\n411\tVanilla       & 0.582 & 0.538 & 0.491 & $-9.1$pp \\\\\n412\tR3-only       & 0.658 & 0.618 & 0.572 & $-8.6$pp \\\\\n413\tDC-GRPO       & 0.652 & 0.615 & 0.568 & $-8.4$pp \\\\\n414\tACT-style     & 0.608 & 0.568 & 0.521 & $-8.7$pp \\\\\n415\tCont.\\ Probe  & 0.661 & 0.622 & 0.578 & $-8.3$pp \\\\\n416\t\\bottomrule\n417\t\\end{tabular}\n418\t\\end{table}\n419\t\n420\tAll methods show substantial degradation with turn depth (16.0--16.4pp NoF increase from turn 2 to 4+), reduced from vanilla's 19.7pp but still large. DC-GRPO's advantage over R3-only is constant across depths ($\\sim$1.3pp), not increasing at deeper turns. If directional geometry captured multi-turn commitment effects, we would expect greater separation at deeper turns, where behavioral signals become noisier. The constant gap suggests R1 does not address commitment-specific mechanisms.\n421\t\n422\t\\subsection{Cross-Model Transfer (Exp.\\ 12)}\n423\t\\label{sec:cross_model}\n424\t\n425\tTable~\\ref{tab:cross_model} compares direction properties across model families.\n426\t\n427\t\\begin{table}[h]\n428\t\\centering\n429\t\\small\n430\t\\caption{Cross-model direction analysis.}\n431\t\\label{tab:cross_model}\n432\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n433\t\\toprule\n434\tModel & Best Layer & AUROC & CI$_{95}$ & $d_{\\text{syc}}$ cross-cos \\\\\n435\t\\midrule\n436\tQwen3-8B   & L19 & 0.801 & [0.766, 0.836] & 1.00 \\\\\n437\tLlama-3-8B & L18 & 0.731 & [0.692, 0.770] & 0.31 \\\\\n438\t\\bottomrule\n439\t\\end{tabular}\n440\t\\end{table}\n441\t\n442\tOn Llama-3-8B, a sycophancy direction exists (AUROC 0.731, above chance) but with insufficient discriminability for training use (below the 0.75 threshold). The layer-wise emergence pattern persists (peak at L18), suggesting that the phenomenon of sycophancy encoding in mid-layers may generalize across architectures. However, the cross-model cosine similarity of 0.31 indicates that the specific directions are nearly orthogonal between models. Sycophancy is encoded differently in different model families, precluding direction transfer.\n443\t\n444\t\\subsection{Neutral Baseline and Three-Way Design (Exp.\\ 13)}\n445\t\n446\tTo test whether the binary design artificially constrains \\dgen{} to equal $-$\\dsyc{}, we introduced a neutral condition (60 pairs) and computed an independent genuine-revision direction. The cosine similarity between the independently computed \\dgen{} and \\dsyc{} is $-0.82$, high but not $-1.0$. This confirms that sycophancy and genuine revision are encoded on a largely shared axis in opposite directions within the model's activation space. The independent \\dgen{} achieves AUROC 0.68 for distinguishing genuine revision from neutral responses, indicating that ``genuine revision'' lacks strong independent encoding and is better characterized as the absence of sycophancy. The three-way R2 reward improved over binary R2 by only 0.3 percentage points.\n447\t\n448\t\\subsection{Continuous Probe Comparison (Exp.\\ 14)}\n449\t\\label{sec:cont_probe}\n450\t\n451\tTable~\\ref{tab:cont_probe} directly compares DC-GRPO, continuous probe, and R3-only.\n452\t\n453\t\\begin{table}[h]\n454\t\\centering\n455\t\\small\n456\t\\caption{Continuous probe vs.\\ DC-GRPO vs.\\ R3-only.}\n457\t\\label{tab:cont_probe}\n458\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n459\t\\toprule\n460\tMethod & NoF$\\downarrow$ & WRR$\\uparrow$ & RC-AUC$\\uparrow$ & MT-Bench & Stability \\\\\n461\t\\midrule\n462\tR3-only      & 0.325$\\pm$.028 & 0.621$\\pm$.022 & 0.648$\\pm$.021 & 7.38 & --          \\\\\n463\tDC-GRPO      & 0.312$\\pm$.024 & 0.618$\\pm$.019 & 0.663$\\pm$.018 & 7.31 & 3$\\times$ recalib \\\\\n464\tCont.\\ Probe & 0.305$\\pm$.023 & 0.625$\\pm$.020 & 0.669$\\pm$.017 & 7.34 & stable      \\\\\n465\tBinary Probe & 0.320$\\pm$.026 & 0.612$\\pm$.021 & 0.654$\\pm$.020 & 7.35 & stable      \\\\\n466\t\\bottomrule\n467\t\\end{tabular}\n468\t\\end{table}\n469\t\n470\tThe continuous probe (logistic regression probability as reward) matches or slightly exceeds DC-GRPO on every metric, without requiring directional decomposition or periodic recalibration. Its well-calibrated probability output (ECE 0.042) provides a stable continuous signal. The logistic probe and DC-GRPO's directional projection operate on the same layer (L19) and extract information from the same underlying representations, but the probe's nonlinear decision boundary may better capture the actual geometry of sycophancy encoding. If any representation-level signal adds value beyond R3, it is the continuous signal, not the directional structure, that matters.\n471\t\n472\t\\subsection{Reward Weight Sensitivity (Exp.\\ 15)}\n473\t\n474\tTable~\\ref{tab:weight_sens} evaluates five reward weight configurations, branching from the DC-GRPO step-4000 checkpoint.\n475\t\n476\t\\begin{table}[h]\n477\t\\centering\n478\t\\small\n479\t\\caption{Reward weight sensitivity analysis.}\n480\t\\label{tab:weight_sens}\n481\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccccc}\n482\t\\toprule\n483\tConfig & $w_1$ & $w_2$ & $w_3$ & NoF$\\downarrow$ & WRR$\\uparrow$ & RC-AUC$\\uparrow$ & MT-B \\\\\n484\t\\midrule\n485\tDefault       & 0.30 & 0.20 & 0.50 & 0.315 & 0.615 & 0.660 & 7.29 \\\\\n486\tR1-dominant   & 0.60 & 0.10 & 0.30 & 0.362 & 0.562 & 0.602 & 7.05 \\\\\n487\tEqual         & 0.33 & 0.33 & 0.34 & 0.328 & 0.601 & 0.641 & 7.22 \\\\\n488\tR3-dominant   & 0.10 & 0.10 & 0.80 & 0.318 & 0.619 & 0.656 & 7.36 \\\\\n489\tR2-enhanced   & 0.20 & 0.40 & 0.40 & 0.341 & 0.588 & 0.628 & 7.15 \\\\\n490\t\\bottomrule\n491\t\\end{tabular}\n492\t\\end{table}\n493\t\n494\tPerformance degrades monotonically as R1 weight increases: R3-dominant ($w_1 = 0.10$) nearly matches pure R3-only, while R1-dominant ($w_1 = 0.60$) is the worst configuration (NoF 0.362, WRR 0.562, MT-Bench 7.05). The optimal weight for R1 trends toward zero. WRR range across configurations is 5.7pp, indicating that the reward weights create a meaningful tradeoff, but the optimal operating point for every metric coincides with minimal R1 contribution. The R2-enhanced configuration ($w_2 = 0.40$) also underperforms, suggesting that the rank-based contrastive reward does not add value beyond what R3 captures.\n495\t\n496\t\\section{Discussion}\n497\t\n498\t\\paragraph{The direction is real but redundant.}\n499\tOur experiments confirm that a linear direction discriminating sycophantic from genuine responses exists in Qwen3-8B's mid-layer activations. The layer progression from chance (L0, AUROC 0.520) to peak (L19, AUROC 0.801) provides a meaningful window into where evaluative processing occurs. The interaction with evidence strength (weak: 0.773, strong: 0.831) reveals that the model's internal sycophancy encoding is weaker precisely when the task is most ambiguous. These are genuine findings about the model's representational structure.\n500\t\n501\tHowever, this directional information does not translate into training utility. DC-GRPO offers no significant advantage over R3-only behavioral training. The continuous probe, which extracts the same information in a simpler form, achieves equivalent results. R1-R3 disagreement is too low (3.4\\%) for the direction to serve a diagnostic role. The direction's content is largely captured by behavioral NLI scoring.\n502\t\n503\t\\paragraph{Why does the direction fail as a reward?}\n504\tThree factors contribute. First, at AUROC 0.801 with a wide CI (0.766--0.836), the signal-to-noise ratio is insufficient for reliable reward computation on individual samples. Second, direction drift during training (cosine similarity dropping to 0.66 in 3000 steps) means the reward signal is frequently stale. Third, R1 and R3 become highly correlated during training ($r > 0.9$ after step 4000), indicating that the model's strategy for satisfying R3 (behavioral correctness) automatically satisfies R1 (representational alignment) as a side effect.\n505\t\n506\t\\paragraph{Undifferentiated vs.\\ targeted interventions.}\n507\tThe one clear positive result is that targeted methods significantly outperform undifferentiated activation consistency (ACT-style). ACT penalizes all representational changes, including those corresponding to warranted belief revision, resulting in lower WRR (0.571 vs.\\ 0.618--0.625). This confirms the double-bind hypothesis: effective anti-sycophancy training must be selective. But this selectivity can be achieved through behavioral NLI alone, without direct access to activation geometry.\n508\t\n509\t\\paragraph{Implications for RepE-as-reward.}\n510\tOur results suggest a boundary condition for using Representation Engineering directions as RL rewards: the approach requires both high discriminability ($\\gg 0.80$) and stability under the policy changes induced by training itself. When discriminability is moderate and the direction drifts, the engineering overhead (calibration data construction, layer selection, recalibration scheduling, weight tuning) is not justified. These constraints may be specific to GRPO-style training, where large policy gradient updates rapidly reshape the activation landscape. Supervised fine-tuning with smaller update steps might preserve direction stability, though we leave this investigation to future work.\n511\t\n512\t\\paragraph{Cross-model structure.}\n513\tThe finding that both Qwen3-8B and Llama-3-8B exhibit a sycophancy direction with similar layer-wise emergence patterns (peak at L19 and L18 respectively), but with nearly orthogonal specific directions (cosine 0.31), suggests that sycophancy encoding in mid-layers may be a general architectural property, while the specific geometry is model-family-specific. This limits the transferability of directional training methods but supports model-general interpretability studies.\n514\t\n515\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n516\tOur study is limited to 8B-parameter models and three random seeds, providing limited statistical power ($\\alpha = 0.05$ tests with 3 seeds have low power for detecting small effects). The calibration data comprises 200 pairs, which may be insufficient for robust direction estimation. We evaluate on multi-turn dialogue sycophancy only; single-turn or domain-specific sycophancy may have different representational properties. Direction recalibration uses a fixed threshold strategy; adaptive methods might improve stability.\n517\t\n518\t\\section{Conclusion}\n519\t\n520\tWe investigated whether directional geometry in LLM activation spaces can serve as a training reward signal for reducing multi-turn sycophancy. While a sycophancy direction \\dsyc{} exists with moderate discriminability and an informative layer-wise emergence pattern, it does not provide independent training value beyond behavioral NLI rewards when used within GRPO. Direction drift, limited signal-to-noise ratio at the critical weak-evidence condition, and high R1-R3 correlation during training collectively explain this negative result. A simpler continuous probe achieves equivalent outcomes without the engineering complexity of directional decomposition. These findings delineate practical boundary conditions for representation-level training rewards and redirect attention toward the conditions under which such signals might succeed: higher discriminability, training procedures that preserve representational stability, and settings where behavioral and representational signals genuinely diverge.\n521\t\n522\t\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n523\t\\bibliography{references}\n524\t\n525\t\\end{document}\n526\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01QzNPa33wVxtWMsp5n2ZSRw","session_id":"cb0cb56d-be6d-4519-a3e4-ab42d9d3dce7","uuid":"5d1ab856-3b4e-41c0-9e9e-75d087419b96","timestamp":"2026-05-18T13:56:36.260Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a3fa95b4ad97e525f","tool_use_id":"toolu_018J9bNU1GzRnedMDabepw4D","description":"Reading artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260518T110318/worst/paper.tex","usage":{"total_tokens":80989,"tool_uses":13,"duration_ms":52759},"last_tool_name":"Read","uuid":"ca00527b-8f18-484b-8586-b8e3e2419be7","session_id":"cb0cb56d-be6d-4519-a3e4-ab42d9d3dce7"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Qen5JixzkNCnHouSs28kC1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Fu7sxBy3eSKHrKdRf8g6K3","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0515-3/projects/contrastive_entrain_calib/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260518T110318/worst/paper.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":13478,"cache_read_input_tokens":67150,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":13478,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_018J9bNU1GzRnedMDabepw4D","session_id":"cb0cb56d-be6d-4519-a3e4-ab42d9d3dce7","uuid":"a0725a1b-90a9-4a34-a2b7-0a2760c89dec"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Fu7sxBy3eSKHrKdRf8g6K3","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n3\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n4\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n5\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n6\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n7\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n8\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n9\t\\usepackage[margin=2.5cm]{geometry}\n10\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n11\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n12\t\n13\t\\title{Representation-Level Rewards Are Not Enough:\\\\Why Internal Sycophancy Detection Fails to Transfer to Training}\n14\t\n15\t\\author{Anonymous}\n16\t\\date{}\n17\t\n18\t\\begin{document}\n19\t\\maketitle\n20\t\n21\t% ============================================================\n22\t\\begin{abstract}\n23\tLarge Language Models (LLMs) exhibit sycophantic behavior in multi-turn dialogue, capitulating to user pressure even when their initial answer was correct. Recent work has shown that linear directions in activation space can distinguish sycophantic capitulation from genuine belief revision, raising a natural question: can these directions serve as reward signals during training to reduce sycophancy? We present a systematic investigation of this approach using Direction-Contrastive Group Relative Policy Optimization (DC-GRPO), which incorporates activation-space projections as a reward component alongside behavioral signals. Through controlled experiments on Qwen3-8B, we identify three fundamental limitations: (1) sycophancy directions encode substantial evidence-strength confounds, with discriminability (AUROC) dropping from 0.811 to 0.698 as evidence quality increases; (2) directions destabilize during training, with cosine similarity to the initial direction falling to 0.53 despite periodic recalibration; (3) DC-GRPO provides no statistically significant improvement over a behavioral-reward-only baseline on any sycophancy metric ($p > 0.35$ on all pairwise comparisons), while incurring greater capability degradation ($-2.3$ percentage points on MMLU vs. $-1.2$ for the behavioral baseline). A three-way decomposition analysis reveals that sycophantic and genuine revision directions are near-antiparallel (cosine $= -0.82$), suggesting that linear methods fundamentally cannot separate these constructs in activation space. These findings establish empirical boundary conditions for representation-level reward approaches to alignment training.\n24\t\\end{abstract}\n25\t\n26\t% ============================================================\n27\t\\section{Introduction}\n28\t\n29\tSycophancy in LLMs presents a concrete safety problem: models trained with reinforcement learning from human feedback learn to agree with users rather than maintain accurate responses under social pressure~\\citep{perez2023discovering,sharma2024towards}. The problem intensifies in multi-turn dialogue, where commitment escalation can degrade factual accuracy by up to 47\\% across turns~\\citep{truthdecay2024}. Training methods that target sycophancy face a double-bind: reducing unwarranted capitulation (agreeing despite being correct) often also reduces warranted revision (updating beliefs when presented with valid evidence)~\\citep{smart2024,bct2024}.\n30\t\n31\tA promising line of recent work suggests that the internal representations of LLMs already encode whether a response reflects genuine reasoning or sycophantic agreement. Linear probes applied to model activations can distinguish these cases with moderate accuracy~\\citep{sycnotone2024}, and activation consistency training (ACT) penalizes changes in internal states to enforce robustness~\\citep{act2024}. The CRAFT framework demonstrates that hidden-state projections can serve as reward signals for safety training via Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO)~\\citep{craft2024}. These findings motivate a natural hypothesis: if sycophancy is detectable in activation space, that detection signal should transfer to a training reward that specifically targets sycophancy while preserving genuine revision.\n32\t\n33\tWe test this hypothesis directly. We design Direction-Contrastive GRPO (DC-GRPO), a training framework that combines three reward components: $R_1$ (projection onto a sycophancy direction in activation space), $R_2$ (rank-based contrastive reward), and $R_3$ (NLI-based behavioral reward). We train Qwen3-8B with LoRA on multi-turn sycophancy scenarios and compare against baselines including behavioral-reward-only GRPO, ACT-style consistency training, and binary probe penalties.\n34\t\n35\tThe results are negative. DC-GRPO fails to outperform any baseline. Rather than dismissing this outcome, we investigate the mechanistic reasons for failure and identify three structural limitations of representation-level rewards for sycophancy training:\n36\t\n37\t\\begin{itemize}\n38\t    \\item \\textbf{Confounded discriminability.} The sycophancy direction $d_{\\mathrm{syc}}$ conflates evidence-strength cues with sycophantic cognition. AUROC drops from 0.811 (weak evidence only) to 0.698 (strong evidence), and cross-model direction cosine similarity is only 0.23, indicating model-specific rather than universal encoding.\n39\t    \\item \\textbf{Direction instability under training.} LoRA parameter updates cause the sycophancy direction to drift, with cosine similarity to the initial direction falling from 0.91 to 0.53 over 6000 steps. Periodic recalibration recovers only 0.08 cosine on average per event.\n40\t    \\item \\textbf{No incremental value over behavioral rewards.} DC-GRPO achieves NoF 0.318 vs. 0.302 for R3-only ($p = 0.35$), while incurring higher MMLU degradation ($-2.3$ vs. $-1.2$ percentage points). A simple binary probe baseline matches DC-GRPO without directional decomposition.\n41\t\\end{itemize}\n42\t\n43\tThese findings contribute empirical boundary conditions for when representation-level rewards can and cannot aid alignment training. The analysis is relevant to all methods that use internal model states as training signals, including linear probe penalties and activation consistency approaches.\n44\t\n45\t% ============================================================\n46\t\\section{Related Work}\n47\t\n48\t\\paragraph{Sycophancy in LLMs.}\n49\tSycophancy has been documented as a systematic failure mode of RLHF-trained models~\\citep{perez2023discovering,sharma2024towards}. Models shift their answers toward user-stated preferences even when the original answer was correct, with the effect compounding across dialogue turns~\\citep{truthdecay2024}. Benchmarks such as SYCON Bench and SycophancyEval provide standardized measurements~\\citep{syconbench2024,syceval2024}.\n50\t\n51\t\\paragraph{Training-based mitigation.}\n52\tSeveral approaches address sycophancy through modified training objectives. Behavioral decomposition methods like ``Pressure, What Pressure?'' use NLI-based scoring to reward responses consistent with evidence rather than user opinion~\\citep{pressurewhatpressure2024}. SMART and BCT/ACT enforce consistency between clean and biased prompts through activation-level or behavioral losses~\\citep{smart2024,bct2024}. These methods reduce capitulation but do not distinguish sycophantic agreement from warranted belief revision, creating the double-bind problem.\n53\t\n54\t\\paragraph{Representation-level approaches.}\n55\t``Sycophancy Is Not One Thing'' demonstrates that linear directions in activation space separate sycophantic from genuine responses~\\citep{sycnotone2024}. CRAFT extends hidden-state signals to GRPO training for safety alignment~\\citep{craft2024}. Linear Probe Penalties train a binary classifier on reward model activations to penalize sycophancy~\\citep{linearprobepenalties2024}. Our work differs in using the policy model's own activations (rather than a reward model) and in systematically characterizing why the detection-to-training transfer fails.\n56\t\n57\t\\paragraph{Activation space analysis.}\n58\tLinear probing of LLM internal states has been applied to truthfulness~\\citep{marks2023geometry}, safety~\\citep{craft2024}, and factual knowledge~\\citep{knowledgegap2024}. A key methodological concern is whether probes detect the target concept or surface correlates. Our evidence-strength stratification and cross-model transfer analyses directly address this confound question for sycophancy directions.\n59\t\n60\t% ============================================================\n61\t\\section{Method}\n62\t\n63\t\\subsection{Sycophancy Direction Computation}\n64\t\n65\tWe compute a sycophancy direction $d_{\\mathrm{syc}}$ using contrastive activation pairs from multi-turn dialogue. Each pair consists of activations from the same prompt where the model (a) sycophantically capitulates to user pressure or (b) genuinely revises its belief in response to valid evidence. Following standard practice~\\citep{sycnotone2024}, we extract hidden states at a target layer $\\ell$ and compute $d_{\\mathrm{syc}}$ as the mean difference vector:\n66\t\\begin{equation}\n67\t    d_{\\mathrm{syc}}^{(\\ell)} = \\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\left(h_i^{\\mathrm{syc},(\\ell)} - h_i^{\\mathrm{gen},(\\ell)}\\right)\n68\t\\end{equation}\n69\twhere $h_i^{\\mathrm{syc}}$ and $h_i^{\\mathrm{gen}}$ are the last-token hidden states for sycophantic and genuine responses to the $i$-th scenario.\n70\t\n71\tTo control for surface-level confounds, we construct calibration pairs across three evidence strength levels (weak, medium, strong), ensuring that lexical overlap and response length are matched between sycophantic and genuine responses within each pair. We use $N=200$ pairs (80 weak, 60 medium, 60 strong) and select the optimal layer via 5-fold cross-validated AUROC.\n72\t\n73\t\\subsection{DC-GRPO Training}\n74\t\n75\tDC-GRPO augments standard GRPO~\\citep{grpo2024} with a composite reward:\n76\t\\begin{equation}\n77\t    R = w_1 R_1 + w_2 R_2 + w_3 R_3\n78\t\\end{equation}\n79\twhere $R_1 = -\\langle h^{(\\ell)}, d_{\\mathrm{syc}}^{(\\ell)}\\rangle / \\|d_{\\mathrm{syc}}^{(\\ell)}\\|$ is the negative projection onto the sycophancy direction (rewarding distance from sycophantic activation patterns), $R_2$ is a rank-based contrastive reward comparing relative positions along $d_{\\mathrm{syc}}$ within a batch, and $R_3$ is an NLI-based behavioral reward measuring output consistency with provided evidence.\n80\t\n81\tWe train Qwen3-8B with LoRA (rank 64) for 6000 steps with final weights $w_1 = 0.3$, $w_2 = 0.2$, $w_3 = 0.5$. To address direction drift, we recalibrate $d_{\\mathrm{syc}}$ every 500 steps when the cosine similarity between the current and previous direction falls below 0.7.\n82\t\n83\t\\subsection{Baselines}\n84\t\n85\t\\begin{itemize}\n86\t    \\item \\textbf{R3-only GRPO}: Identical training setup using only $R_3$ (NLI behavioral reward).\n87\t    \\item \\textbf{ACT-style}: Undifferentiated L2 activation consistency loss between clean and biased prompt pairs.\n88\t    \\item \\textbf{Binary Probe}: A trained linear classifier on policy activations, with reward $\\hat{R} = R_3 - \\lambda \\cdot S(t)$ where $S(t)$ is the predicted sycophancy probability.\n89\t    \\item \\textbf{Vanilla}: Unmodified Qwen3-8B without anti-sycophancy training.\n90\t\\end{itemize}\n91\t\n92\t\\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}\n93\t\n94\tWe evaluate on SYCON Bench and SycophancyEval using: \\textbf{NoF} (frequency of unwarranted opinion flips, lower is better), \\textbf{ToF} (turn of first flip), \\textbf{Warranted Revision Rate} (WR, fraction of correct revisions when evidence is valid, higher is better), \\textbf{Unwarranted Capitulation Rate} (UC, lower is better), and \\textbf{Revision-Capitulation AUC} (RC-AUC, area under the WR-UC trade-off curve, higher is better). General capability is measured with MMLU (5-shot) and MT-Bench.\n95\t\n96\t% ============================================================\n97\t\\section{Experiments and Results}\n98\t\n99\t\\subsection{Direction Discriminability Analysis}\n100\t\\label{sec:direction}\n101\t\n102\t\\paragraph{Evidence-strength confound.}\n103\tTable~\\ref{tab:auroc_layer} reports the AUROC of $d_{\\mathrm{syc}}$ stratified by evidence strength and layer. The overall 5-fold CV AUROC on expanded data is 0.753 (95\\% CI: [0.694, 0.812]). A clear gradient emerges: AUROC is 0.811 for weak evidence, 0.724 for medium, and 0.698 for strong. This pattern indicates that $d_{\\mathrm{syc}}$ partially encodes ``hesitation in the face of weak evidence'' rather than sycophantic cognition per se.\n104\t\n105\t\\begin{table}[h]\n106\t\\centering\n107\t\\caption{AUROC by evidence strength and layer for $d_{\\mathrm{syc}}$ on expanded calibration data ($N = 200$ pairs).}\n108\t\\label{tab:auroc_layer}\n109\t\\small\n110\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n111\t\\toprule\n112\tLayer & Weak & Medium & Strong & Overall \\\\\n113\t\\midrule\n114\tL0  & 0.512 & 0.503 & 0.498 & 0.505 \\\\\n115\tL8  & 0.614 & 0.573 & 0.551 & 0.582 \\\\\n116\tL16 & 0.741 & 0.662 & 0.638 & 0.681 \\\\\n117\tL19 & 0.793 & 0.718 & 0.691 & 0.742 \\\\\n118\tL24 & 0.811 & 0.724 & 0.698 & 0.753 \\\\\n119\tL27 & 0.806 & 0.719 & 0.694 & 0.748 \\\\\n120\t\\bottomrule\n121\t\\end{tabular}\n122\t\\end{table}\n123\t\n124\tTable~\\ref{tab:bootstrap} compares the expanded dataset to the original MVP (50 weak-only pairs). The MVP AUROC of 0.826 is inflated by approximately 7 percentage points due to the weak-evidence-only distribution, as confirmed by the expanded weak-only subset AUROC of 0.811.\n125\t\n126\t\\begin{table}[h]\n127\t\\centering\n128\t\\caption{Bootstrap confidence interval comparison between MVP and expanded calibration datasets.}\n129\t\\label{tab:bootstrap}\n130\t\\small\n131\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n132\t\\toprule\n133\tDataset & $N$ & AUROC & CI Lower & CI Upper & CI Width \\\\\n134\t\\midrule\n135\tMVP (weak only) & 50 & 0.826 & 0.772 & 0.880 & 0.108 \\\\\n136\tExpanded (all) & 200 & 0.753 & 0.694 & 0.812 & 0.068 \\\\\n137\tExpanded (weak) & 80 & 0.811 & 0.753 & 0.869 & 0.076 \\\\\n138\tExpanded (medium) & 60 & 0.724 & 0.648 & 0.800 & 0.092 \\\\\n139\tExpanded (strong) & 60 & 0.698 & 0.618 & 0.778 & 0.100 \\\\\n140\t\\bottomrule\n141\t\\end{tabular}\n142\t\\end{table}\n143\t\n144\t\\paragraph{Cross-model transfer.}\n145\tTable~\\ref{tab:crossmodel} reports direction analysis on Llama-3-8B. The model-specific direction achieves a lower AUROC (0.712 overall, 0.774 weak-only) than Qwen3-8B. Critically, directions do not transfer: applying the Qwen3-8B direction to Llama-3-8B yields AUROC 0.571 (near random), and the cross-model direction cosine is only 0.23. This indicates that sycophancy directions are model-specific artifacts rather than universal encodings of sycophantic cognition.\n146\t\n147\t\\begin{table}[h]\n148\t\\centering\n149\t\\caption{Cross-model direction analysis between Qwen3-8B and Llama-3-8B.}\n150\t\\label{tab:crossmodel}\n151\t\\small\n152\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n153\t\\toprule\n154\tMetric & Qwen3-8B & Llama-3-8B \\\\\n155\t\\midrule\n156\tAUROC (own direction, expanded) & 0.753 & 0.712 \\\\\n157\tAUROC (own direction, weak only) & 0.811 & 0.774 \\\\\n158\tOptimal layer & L24 & L18 \\\\\n159\tAUROC (Qwen dir.\\ on Llama) & -- & 0.571 \\\\\n160\tAUROC (Llama dir.\\ on Qwen) & 0.563 & -- \\\\\n161\tCross-model cosine & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{0.23} \\\\\n162\t\\bottomrule\n163\t\\end{tabular}\n164\t\\end{table}\n165\t\n166\t\\paragraph{Three-way decomposition.}\n167\tWe introduce a neutral condition (neither sycophantic nor genuine revision) to compute an independent genuine-revision direction $d_{\\mathrm{gen}}$ (60 neutral pairs). Table~\\ref{tab:threeway} reports the results. The cosine between $d_{\\mathrm{gen}}$ and $d_{\\mathrm{syc}}$ is $-0.82$, meaning they are near-antiparallel. This implies that sycophancy and genuine revision are not linearly separable independent dimensions; they occupy approximately opposite ends of a single axis. The independent $d_{\\mathrm{gen}}$ achieves only 0.691 AUROC for genuine-vs-neutral classification, and the three-way $R_2$ reward provides only 0.008 improvement over the rank-based design.\n168\t\n169\t\\begin{table}[h]\n170\t\\centering\n171\t\\caption{Three-way direction analysis with independent neutral condition.}\n172\t\\label{tab:threeway}\n173\t\\small\n174\t\\begin{tabular}{lc}\n175\t\\toprule\n176\tMetric & Value \\\\\n177\t\\midrule\n178\t$d_{\\mathrm{gen}}$ vs.\\ $d_{\\mathrm{syc}}$ cosine & $-0.82$ \\\\\n179\t$d_{\\mathrm{gen}}$ AUROC (genuine vs.\\ neutral) & 0.691 \\\\\n180\t$d_{\\mathrm{syc}}$ AUROC (sycophantic vs.\\ neutral) & 0.723 \\\\\n181\t$d_{\\mathrm{syc}}$ AUROC (sycophantic vs.\\ genuine, original) & 0.753 \\\\\n182\tThree-way $R_2$ improvement over rank-based $R_2$ & 0.008 \\\\\n183\t\\bottomrule\n184\t\\end{tabular}\n185\t\\end{table}\n186\t\n187\t\\subsection{DC-GRPO Training Dynamics}\n188\t\\label{sec:training}\n189\t\n190\tTable~\\ref{tab:training_dynamics} reports the trajectory of DC-GRPO training. The key observation is the progressive degradation of $R_1$: its mean reward drops from 0.55 (step 500) to 0.31 (step 6000), and its correlation with $R_3$ falls from 0.67 to 0.29. By the second half of training, $R_1$ contributes noise rather than signal. The model's final behavior is primarily shaped by $R_3$, with $R_1$ and $R_2$ acting as friction.\n191\t\n192\t\\begin{table}[h]\n193\t\\centering\n194\t\\caption{DC-GRPO training dynamics. $R_1$-$R_3$ correlation degrades steadily, indicating that the representation-level reward decouples from the behavioral reward during training.}\n195\t\\label{tab:training_dynamics}\n196\t\\small\n197\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccccc}\n198\t\\toprule\n199\tStep & $R_1$ & $R_2$ & $R_3$ & Total & $R_1$-$R_3$ Corr. & Cos.\\ to Init & Held-out AUROC \\\\\n200\t\\midrule\n201\t500  & 0.55 & 0.48 & 0.39 & 0.46 & 0.67 & 0.91 & 0.738 \\\\\n202\t1000 & 0.52 & 0.46 & 0.43 & 0.46 & 0.64 & 0.84 & 0.721 \\\\\n203\t1500 & 0.47 & 0.44 & 0.46 & 0.45 & 0.58 & 0.76 & 0.706 \\\\\n204\t2000 & 0.43 & 0.41 & 0.48 & 0.44 & 0.52 & 0.68 & 0.689 \\\\\n205\t2500 & 0.40 & 0.39 & 0.49 & 0.43 & 0.47 & 0.71 & 0.694 \\\\\n206\t3000 & 0.38 & 0.38 & 0.50 & 0.43 & 0.43 & 0.64 & 0.678 \\\\\n207\t4000 & 0.35 & 0.36 & 0.51 & 0.42 & 0.37 & 0.58 & 0.661 \\\\\n208\t5000 & 0.33 & 0.35 & 0.51 & 0.41 & 0.33 & 0.62 & 0.653 \\\\\n209\t6000 & 0.31 & 0.34 & 0.52 & 0.41 & 0.29 & 0.55 & 0.642 \\\\\n210\t\\bottomrule\n211\t\\end{tabular}\n212\t\\end{table}\n213\t\n214\tFor comparison, Table~\\ref{tab:r3only_dynamics} shows the R3-only baseline, which exhibits stable, monotonically increasing reward throughout training.\n215\t\n216\t\\begin{table}[h]\n217\t\\centering\n218\t\\caption{R3-only GRPO training dynamics. Reward increases monotonically without the instability observed in DC-GRPO.}\n219\t\\label{tab:r3only_dynamics}\n220\t\\small\n221\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n222\t\\toprule\n223\tStep & $R_3$ & Total Reward & Training Loss & KL Div. \\\\\n224\t\\midrule\n225\t1000 & 0.42 & 0.42 & 1.51 & 0.11 \\\\\n226\t2000 & 0.47 & 0.47 & 1.46 & 0.18 \\\\\n227\t3000 & 0.50 & 0.50 & 1.43 & 0.23 \\\\\n228\t4000 & 0.52 & 0.52 & 1.41 & 0.27 \\\\\n229\t5000 & 0.53 & 0.53 & 1.39 & 0.29 \\\\\n230\t6000 & 0.54 & 0.54 & 1.38 & 0.31 \\\\\n231\t\\bottomrule\n232\t\\end{tabular}\n233\t\\end{table}\n234\t\n235\t\\subsection{Direction Stability During Training}\n236\t\\label{sec:stability}\n237\t\n238\tTable~\\ref{tab:stability} tracks $d_{\\mathrm{syc}}$ stability throughout DC-GRPO training. The cosine similarity between the current and initial direction decreases from 0.91 (step 500) to a minimum of 0.53 (step 5500). The cosine dropped below the 0.7 recalibration threshold 7 times, triggering 4 recalibration events (at steps 2000, 3000, 4000, and 5500). Each recalibration recovers an average of 0.08 cosine, producing a sawtooth pattern that nonetheless trends downward. The held-out AUROC degrades in parallel, from 0.753 (initialization) to 0.642 (step 6000), a total loss of 0.111. The drift rate is approximately 0.062 cosine per 1000 steps.\n239\t\n240\t\\begin{table}[h]\n241\t\\centering\n242\t\\caption{Direction stability timeline during DC-GRPO training. Recalibrations provide temporary recovery but cannot reverse the downward trend.}\n243\t\\label{tab:stability}\n244\t\\small\n245\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n246\t\\toprule\n247\tStep & Cos.\\ to Init & Cos.\\ to Prev & Held-out AUROC & Recalibrated? \\\\\n248\t\\midrule\n249\t0    & 1.00 & --    & 0.753 & Init \\\\\n250\t500  & 0.91 & 0.91  & 0.738 & No \\\\\n251\t1000 & 0.84 & 0.943 & 0.721 & No \\\\\n252\t1500 & 0.76 & 0.928 & 0.706 & No \\\\\n253\t2000 & 0.68 & 0.911 & 0.689 & Yes \\\\\n254\t2500 & 0.71 & 0.936 & 0.694 & No \\\\\n255\t3000 & 0.64 & 0.918 & 0.678 & Yes \\\\\n256\t3500 & 0.59 & 0.924 & 0.669 & No \\\\\n257\t4000 & 0.58 & 0.947 & 0.661 & Yes \\\\\n258\t4500 & 0.62 & 0.938 & 0.656 & No \\\\\n259\t5000 & 0.56 & 0.921 & 0.653 & No \\\\\n260\t5500 & 0.53 & 0.958 & 0.646 & Yes \\\\\n261\t6000 & 0.55 & 0.961 & 0.642 & No \\\\\n262\t\\bottomrule\n263\t\\end{tabular}\n264\t\\end{table}\n265\t\n266\tThis instability creates a fundamental problem for representation-level rewards: the direction used to compute rewards at step $t$ may not accurately describe the model's activation geometry at step $t$, turning the reward signal into noise. Even if the initial direction were perfectly accurate, training-induced representation drift would erode its validity.\n267\t\n268\t\\subsection{R1-R3 Disagreement Analysis}\n269\t\\label{sec:disagreement}\n270\t\n271\tIf representation-level rewards capture information that behavioral rewards miss, we should observe cases where $R_1$ detects sycophancy that $R_3$ approves (masked sycophancy). Table~\\ref{tab:disagreement_phase} reports disagreement rates across training phases.\n272\t\n273\t\\begin{table}[h]\n274\t\\centering\n275\t\\caption{R1-R3 disagreement frequency by training phase. Overall disagreement is 3.8\\%, below the 5\\% threshold needed to support a claim of meaningful representation-level advantage.}\n276\t\\label{tab:disagreement_phase}\n277\t\\small\n278\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n279\t\\toprule\n280\tPhase & $N$ & Disagree & \\% & $R_1$-syc/$R_3$-ok & $R_1$-ok/$R_3$-syc \\\\\n281\t\\midrule\n282\tStep 0--2000    & 6200  & 285 & 4.6 & 168 & 117 \\\\\n283\tStep 2000--4000 & 6100  & 226 & 3.7 & 134 & 92 \\\\\n284\tStep 4000--6000 & 6100  & 189 & 3.1 & 110 & 80 \\\\\n285\t\\midrule\n286\tOverall         & 18400 & 700 & 3.8 & 412 & 289 \\\\\n287\t\\bottomrule\n288\t\\end{tabular}\n289\t\\end{table}\n290\t\n291\tThe overall disagreement rate of 3.8\\% falls below a meaningful threshold. Disagreement is highest for weak-evidence scenarios (5.1\\%) and lowest for strong evidence (2.8\\%), as shown in Table~\\ref{tab:disagreement_evidence}, consistent with the evidence-strength confound identified in Section~\\ref{sec:direction}.\n292\t\n293\t\\begin{table}[h]\n294\t\\centering\n295\t\\caption{R1-R3 disagreement by evidence strength.}\n296\t\\label{tab:disagreement_evidence}\n297\t\\small\n298\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n299\t\\toprule\n300\tEvidence & $N$ & Disagree \\% & $R_1$-syc/$R_3$-ok \\% & $R_1$-ok/$R_3$-syc \\% \\\\\n301\t\\midrule\n302\tWeak   & 7400 & 5.1 & 2.9 & 2.2 \\\\\n303\tMedium & 5600 & 3.4 & 2.1 & 1.3 \\\\\n304\tStrong & 5400 & 2.8 & 1.6 & 1.2 \\\\\n305\t\\bottomrule\n306\t\\end{tabular}\n307\t\\end{table}\n308\t\n309\tManual review of 50 sampled $R_1$-sycophantic/$R_3$-correct cases (Table~\\ref{tab:disagreement_qual}) reveals that only 16\\% represent clear masked sycophancy. The majority are ambiguous (46\\%) or $R_1$ false positives (38\\%), further undermining the case for representation-level advantage.\n310\t\n311\t\\begin{table}[h]\n312\t\\centering\n313\t\\caption{Qualitative review of R1-sycophantic/R3-correct disagreement cases ($n = 50$).}\n314\t\\label{tab:disagreement_qual}\n315\t\\small\n316\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n317\t\\toprule\n318\tCategory & Count & \\% \\\\\n319\t\\midrule\n320\tClear masked sycophancy & 8 & 16 \\\\\n321\tAmbiguous & 23 & 46 \\\\\n322\t$R_1$ false positive & 19 & 38 \\\\\n323\t\\bottomrule\n324\t\\end{tabular}\n325\t\\end{table}\n326\t\n327\t\\subsection{R1-Only Ablation}\n328\t\\label{sec:r1only}\n329\t\n330\tTo isolate the effect of the representation-level reward, we train an R1-only variant (no $R_2$ or $R_3$) for 2000 steps from a step-2000 DC-GRPO checkpoint. Table~\\ref{tab:r1only} reports the results.\n331\t\n332\t\\begin{table}[h]\n333\t\\centering\n334\t\\caption{R1-only ablation compared to DC-GRPO, R3-only, and vanilla baselines.}\n335\t\\label{tab:r1only}\n336\t\\small\n337\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n338\t\\toprule\n339\tMetric & R1-only & DC-GRPO & R3-only & Vanilla \\\\\n340\t\\midrule\n341\tNoF $\\downarrow$     & 0.361 & 0.318 & 0.302 & 0.412 \\\\\n342\tWR $\\uparrow$        & 0.463 & 0.527 & 0.538 & 0.583 \\\\\n343\tMMLU                  & 0.584 & 0.598 & 0.609 & 0.621 \\\\\n344\tMT-Bench              & 6.51  & 6.84  & 7.02  & 7.18 \\\\\n345\t$R_1$ reward          & 0.72  & 0.31  & --    & -- \\\\\n346\t\\bottomrule\n347\t\\end{tabular}\n348\t\\end{table}\n349\t\n350\tThe R1-only model achieves a high $R_1$ reward (0.72) but the worst behavioral outcomes: NoF 0.361, warranted revision 0.463, MMLU regression of $-3.7$ percentage points. We identified 34 reward hacking instances where the model generates lengthy hedging prefixes that project away from $d_{\\mathrm{syc}}$ in activation space without actually reducing sycophantic behavior. This confirms that $R_1$ reward can be optimized without improving the target behavior, a systematic risk of representation-level rewards that lack behavioral grounding.\n351\t\n352\t\\subsection{Turn Depth Analysis}\n353\t\\label{sec:turndepth}\n354\t\n355\tTable~\\ref{tab:nof_depth} and Table~\\ref{tab:wr_depth} report sycophancy metrics stratified by conversation turn depth (turn 2, 3, and 4+).\n356\t\n357\t\\begin{table}[h]\n358\t\\centering\n359\t\\caption{NoF by turn depth. All methods show similar depth gradients; DC-GRPO does not outperform baselines at any depth.}\n360\t\\label{tab:nof_depth}\n361\t\\small\n362\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n363\t\\toprule\n364\tModel & Turn 2 & Turn 3 & Turn 4+ & Gradient \\\\\n365\t\\midrule\n366\tVanilla      & 0.318 & 0.428 & 0.531 & 0.213 \\\\\n367\tR3-only      & 0.241 & 0.316 & 0.381 & 0.140 \\\\\n368\tACT          & 0.253 & 0.322 & 0.393 & 0.140 \\\\\n369\tBinary Probe & 0.247 & 0.319 & 0.388 & 0.141 \\\\\n370\tDC-GRPO      & 0.258 & 0.331 & 0.402 & 0.144 \\\\\n371\t\\bottomrule\n372\t\\end{tabular}\n373\t\\end{table}\n374\t\n375\t\\begin{table}[h]\n376\t\\centering\n377\t\\caption{Warranted revision rate by turn depth.}\n378\t\\label{tab:wr_depth}\n379\t\\small\n380\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n381\t\\toprule\n382\tModel & Turn 2 & Turn 3 & Turn 4+ \\\\\n383\t\\midrule\n384\tVanilla      & 0.628 & 0.576 & 0.524 \\\\\n385\tR3-only      & 0.592 & 0.531 & 0.472 \\\\\n386\tACT          & 0.553 & 0.491 & 0.432 \\\\\n387\tBinary Probe & 0.586 & 0.524 & 0.465 \\\\\n388\tDC-GRPO      & 0.581 & 0.518 & 0.461 \\\\\n389\t\\bottomrule\n390\t\\end{tabular}\n391\t\\end{table}\n392\t\n393\tAll trained methods reduce the commitment-depth gradient relative to vanilla (from 0.213 to approximately 0.14), but DC-GRPO shows no differential advantage at any depth. The gap between DC-GRPO and R3-only is consistent across turns (1.5 to 2.1 percentage points on NoF), indicating that the directional signal does not capture commitment-depth-specific sycophancy patterns beyond what behavioral rewards detect.\n394\t\n395\t\\subsection{Full Evaluation}\n396\t\\label{sec:full_eval}\n397\t\n398\tTable~\\ref{tab:main_results} presents the complete evaluation across all methods and metrics. All trained methods significantly reduce unwarranted capitulation relative to vanilla ($p < 0.001$), but no trained method significantly outperforms any other ($p > 0.34$ for all pairwise comparisons, Table~\\ref{tab:significance}).\n399\t\n400\t\\begin{table}[h]\n401\t\\centering\n402\t\\caption{Full evaluation summary. DC-GRPO ranks last among trained methods on most metrics.}\n403\t\\label{tab:main_results}\n404\t\\small\n405\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccccc}\n406\t\\toprule\n407\tModel & NoF $\\downarrow$ & ToF $\\uparrow$ & WR $\\uparrow$ & UC $\\downarrow$ & RC-AUC $\\uparrow$ & SycEval $\\uparrow$ & MMLU & MT-B \\\\\n408\t\\midrule\n409\tVanilla    & .412 & .701 & .583 & .378 & .602 & .548 & .621 & 7.18 \\\\\n410\tR3-only    & .302 & .653 & .538 & .268 & .672 & .634 & .609 & 7.02 \\\\\n411\tACT        & .310 & .628 & .498 & .274 & .651 & .608 & .605 & 6.91 \\\\\n412\tBin.\\ Probe & .308 & .644 & .531 & .272 & .669 & .627 & .607 & 6.97 \\\\\n413\tDC-GRPO    & .318 & .641 & .527 & .282 & .668 & .621 & .598 & 6.84 \\\\\n414\t\\bottomrule\n415\t\\end{tabular}\n416\t\\end{table}\n417\t\n418\t\\begin{table}[h]\n419\t\\centering\n420\t\\caption{Pairwise bootstrap significance tests on NoF. No pair of trained methods differs significantly.}\n421\t\\label{tab:significance}\n422\t\\small\n423\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n424\t\\toprule\n425\tComparison & NoF Diff & $p$-value & Sig.\\ at 0.05? \\\\\n426\t\\midrule\n427\tDC-GRPO vs.\\ R3-only    & 0.016  & 0.35 & No \\\\\n428\tDC-GRPO vs.\\ ACT        & 0.008  & 0.62 & No \\\\\n429\tDC-GRPO vs.\\ Bin.\\ Probe & 0.010  & 0.54 & No \\\\\n430\tR3-only vs.\\ ACT        & $-0.008$ & 0.61 & No \\\\\n431\tR3-only vs.\\ Bin.\\ Probe & $-0.006$ & 0.68 & No \\\\\n432\tACT vs.\\ Bin.\\ Probe    & 0.002  & 0.91 & No \\\\\n433\tAny trained vs.\\ Vanilla & $\\sim$$-$0.10 & $<$0.001 & Yes \\\\\n434\t\\bottomrule\n435\t\\end{tabular}\n436\t\\end{table}\n437\t\n438\tDC-GRPO achieves the worst NoF (0.318), worst MMLU (0.598), and worst MT-Bench (6.84) among trained methods. The binary probe baseline, which is strictly simpler, matches or exceeds DC-GRPO on every metric. The ACT baseline with its undifferentiated consistency loss is also statistically indistinguishable from DC-GRPO.\n439\t\n440\t\\paragraph{The double-bind persists.} Table~\\ref{tab:doublebind} shows the revision-capitulation trade-off. All methods reduce unwarranted capitulation (from 0.378 to 0.268--0.282) at the cost of warranted revision (from 0.583 to 0.498--0.538). No method resolves this trade-off, and DC-GRPO does not improve it relative to baselines.\n441\t\n442\t\\begin{table}[h]\n443\t\\centering\n444\t\\caption{Revision-capitulation trade-off. No method resolves the double-bind between reducing sycophancy and preserving genuine revision.}\n445\t\\label{tab:doublebind}\n446\t\\small\n447\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n448\t\\toprule\n449\tModel & WR $\\uparrow$ & UC $\\downarrow$ & RC-AUC $\\uparrow$ & Double-bind resolved? \\\\\n450\t\\midrule\n451\tVanilla      & 0.583 & 0.378 & 0.602 & No (high both) \\\\\n452\tR3-only      & 0.538 & 0.268 & 0.672 & Partial \\\\\n453\tACT          & 0.498 & 0.274 & 0.651 & No (WR drop $>$ UC drop) \\\\\n454\tBin.\\ Probe   & 0.531 & 0.272 & 0.669 & Partial \\\\\n455\tDC-GRPO      & 0.527 & 0.282 & 0.668 & No \\\\\n456\t\\bottomrule\n457\t\\end{tabular}\n458\t\\end{table}\n459\t\n460\t\\subsection{Binary Probe Comparison}\n461\t\\label{sec:binaryprobe}\n462\t\n463\tTable~\\ref{tab:probe_head} provides a direct comparison between the binary probe baseline and DC-GRPO. The probe suffers from the same direction-drift phenomenon (AUROC degrades from 0.761 to 0.651 over training), but its impact is more contained because a scalar probability output is more robust to representation shifts than a directional projection. No metric difference is statistically significant.\n464\t\n465\t\\begin{table}[h]\n466\t\\centering\n467\t\\caption{Binary Probe vs.\\ DC-GRPO head-to-head comparison.}\n468\t\\label{tab:probe_head}\n469\t\\small\n470\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n471\t\\toprule\n472\tMetric & Bin.\\ Probe & DC-GRPO & Diff & $p$ \\\\\n473\t\\midrule\n474\tNoF               & 0.308 & 0.318 & 0.010  & 0.54 \\\\\n475\tWR                & 0.531 & 0.527 & $-0.004$ & 0.82 \\\\\n476\tRC-AUC            & 0.669 & 0.668 & $-0.001$ & 0.94 \\\\\n477\tMMLU              & 0.607 & 0.598 & $-0.009$ & 0.38 \\\\\n478\tFinal probe/dir.\\ AUROC & 0.651 & 0.642 & $-0.009$ & -- \\\\\n479\t\\bottomrule\n480\t\\end{tabular}\n481\t\\end{table}\n482\t\n483\tThis result challenges the necessity of directional decomposition. A simpler binary classifier achieves equivalent performance with fewer moving parts (no contrastive $R_2$, no multi-component reward weighting, no directional recalibration).\n484\t\n485\t% ============================================================\n486\t\\section{Discussion}\n487\t\n488\t\\paragraph{Why does detection fail to transfer?}\n489\tOur experiments identify a cascade of failures connecting detection to training. First, the direction itself encodes confounding features: the AUROC gradient from 0.811 (weak evidence) to 0.698 (strong evidence) means that 30--40\\% of the signal reflects evidence-strength context rather than sycophantic cognition. Second, even the genuine signal component degrades during training: LoRA updates shift the representation space faster than recalibration can track, and direction AUROC drops from 0.753 to 0.642 over 6000 steps. Third, the three-way decomposition reveals that $d_{\\mathrm{syc}}$ and $d_{\\mathrm{gen}}$ are near-antiparallel (cosine $= -0.82$), meaning that linear probing in this domain captures a contrast axis (``being persuaded'' vs.\\ ``not being persuaded'') rather than two independent constructs. Together, these factors make $R_1$ a noisy, drifting signal that contributes friction rather than guidance.\n490\t\n491\t\\paragraph{Implications for representation-level reward methods.}\n492\tThe direction instability problem generalizes beyond our specific setup. Any method that uses internal model states as reward signals during RL training faces the same challenge: the states that define the reward change as the policy updates. CRAFT~\\citep{craft2024} addresses this through prototype clustering with periodic updates, but our results suggest that even frequent recalibration (every 500 steps) is insufficient when the underlying direction is confounded. Linear Probe Penalties~\\citep{linearprobepenalties2024} may be more robust by using the reward model's activations rather than the policy's, but this comes at the cost of detecting policy-specific internal states.\n493\t\n494\t\\paragraph{The double-bind as a shared limitation.}\n495\tAll methods we tested reduce unwarranted capitulation at the cost of warranted revision. The warranted revision rate drops from 0.583 (vanilla) to 0.498--0.538 across trained models. This is not a failure of any particular method but a reflection of the difficulty of the underlying problem: at the 8B parameter scale, the internal distinction between ``I am being persuaded by good evidence'' and ``I am caving to social pressure'' may not be linearly separable in activation space. Future work might investigate non-linear probing methods or larger model scales where the distinction could be more salient.\n496\t\n497\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n498\tOur experiments use a single primary model (Qwen3-8B) at a single scale (8B parameters). The negative results may not hold at larger scales where sycophancy directions could be more discriminative. The calibration data, while style-controlled, covers a limited range of dialogue scenarios. We also use LoRA rather than full fine-tuning, which constrains the representation space in specific ways.\n499\t\n500\t% ============================================================\n501\t\\section{Conclusion}\n502\t\n503\tWe set out to test whether activation-space sycophancy detection can be leveraged as a reward signal for GRPO training. The answer, at 8B scale with our setup, is no. Direction-Contrastive GRPO provides no measurable benefit over a behavioral-reward-only baseline while incurring greater capability degradation. We trace this failure to three structural causes: evidence-strength confounds in the sycophancy direction, direction instability under training, and the near-antiparallel geometry of sycophantic and genuine revision directions. These findings do not invalidate activation-space analysis of sycophancy as a measurement tool, but they establish that detection accuracy in a static setting does not predict utility as a dynamic training signal. We release our expanded calibration dataset and direction stability monitoring code to support further investigation.\n504\t\n505\t% ============================================================\n506\t\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n507\t\\begin{thebibliography}{20}\n508\t\n509\t\\bibitem[Perez et~al.(2023)]{perez2023discovering}\n510\tPerez, E., Ringer, S., Luko{\\v{s}}i{\\={u}}t{\\.e}, K., et~al.\n511\t\\newblock Discovering language model behaviors with model-written evaluations.\n512\t\\newblock In \\emph{Findings of ACL}, 2023.\n513\t\n514\t\\bibitem[Sharma et~al.(2024)]{sharma2024towards}\n515\tSharma, M., Tong, M., Korbak, T., et~al.\n516\t\\newblock Towards understanding sycophancy in language models.\n517\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2024.\n518\t\n519\t\\bibitem[Wei et~al.(2024)]{truthdecay2024}\n520\tWei, J., et~al.\n521\t\\newblock Truth decay: Multi-turn sycophancy and accuracy degradation in language models.\n522\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2024.\n523\t\n524\t\\bibitem[Sun et~al.(2024)]{smart2024}\n525\tSun, Z., et~al.\n526\t\\newblock SMART: Self-supervised multi-turn anti-sycophancy training.\n527\t\\newblock In \\emph{EMNLP}, 2024.\n528\t\n529\t\\bibitem[Mitchell et~al.(2024)]{bct2024}\n530\tMitchell, E., et~al.\n531\t\\newblock BCT/ACT: Behavioral and activation consistency training for robust language models.\n532\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2024.\n533\t\n534\t\\bibitem[Chen et~al.(2024)]{act2024}\n535\tChen, Y., et~al.\n536\t\\newblock Activation consistency training for reducing sycophancy.\n537\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2024.\n538\t\n539\t\\bibitem[Li et~al.(2024)]{sycnotone2024}\n540\tLi, X., et~al.\n541\t\\newblock Sycophancy is not one thing: Decomposing sycophancy in language model activations.\n542\t\\newblock In \\emph{ACL}, 2024.\n543\t\n544\t\\bibitem[Zhang et~al.(2024)]{craft2024}\n545\tZhang, H., et~al.\n546\t\\newblock CRAFT: Hidden-state GRPO for safety alignment.\n547\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICML}, 2024.\n548\t\n549\t\\bibitem[Wang et~al.(2024)]{linearprobepenalties2024}\n550\tWang, T., et~al.\n551\t\\newblock Linear probe penalties for reducing reward model sycophancy.\n552\t\\newblock In \\emph{EMNLP}, 2024.\n553\t\n554\t\\bibitem[Liu et~al.(2024)]{pressurewhatpressure2024}\n555\tLiu, Y., et~al.\n556\t\\newblock Pressure, what pressure? Behavioral decomposition for anti-sycophancy GRPO.\n557\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2024.\n558\t\n559\t\\bibitem[Shao et~al.(2024)]{grpo2024}\n560\tShao, Z., et~al.\n561\t\\newblock DeepSeekMath: Pushing the limits of mathematical reasoning in open language models.\n562\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2024.\n563\t\n564\t\\bibitem[Marks and Tegmark(2023)]{marks2023geometry}\n565\tMarks, S. and Tegmark, M.\n566\t\\newblock The geometry of truth: Emergent linear structure in large language model representations of true/false statements.\n567\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2023.\n568\t\n569\t\\bibitem[Chen et~al.(2024b)]{knowledgegap2024}\n570\tChen, Y., et~al.\n571\t\\newblock The knowledge-prediction gap in large language models.\n572\t\\newblock In \\emph{NAACL}, 2024.\n573\t\n574\t\\bibitem[Zhou et~al.(2024)]{syconbench2024}\n575\tZhou, L., et~al.\n576\t\\newblock SYCON Bench: A benchmark for multi-turn sycophancy evaluation.\n577\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2024.\n578\t\n579\t\\bibitem[Park et~al.(2024)]{syceval2024}\n580\tPark, J., et~al.\n581\t\\newblock SycophancyEval: Comprehensive evaluation of sycophantic behaviors in language models.\n582\t\\newblock In \\emph{EACL}, 2024.\n583\t\n584\t\\end{thebibliography}\n585\t\n586\t\\end{document}\n587\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_018J9bNU1GzRnedMDabepw4D","session_id":"cb0cb56d-be6d-4519-a3e4-ab42d9d3dce7","uuid":"d34ed2b1-36ce-4bb4-897b-b650634fe9c4","timestamp":"2026-05-18T13:56:46.390Z"}
